<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where commercial estimates drift</h2>
<p>On a commercial or association claim the estimate can run hundreds of pages. Small unit-price differences multiply across thousands of square feet, and whole trades get left out. Commercial claim appraisal in Vermont means reading the entire file, not just the summary page.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Roof systems.</strong> Membrane, standing-seam metal and slate each carry different tear-off, insulation and detailing costs.</li>
<li><strong>Snow-load and structural repairs.</strong> Collapse or deflection after a heavy, wet snow requires an engineer’s scope, not just a carpenter’s.</li>
<li><strong>Agricultural buildings.</strong> Barns, free-stall housing and sugarhouses often fall under farm forms with their own valuation rules.</li>
<li><strong>Historic downtown blocks.</strong> Brick, cornices and storefronts in places like St. Johnsbury, Brattleboro and Rutland cost more to restore than to replace with generic materials.</li>
<li><strong>General conditions.</strong> Supervision, temporary facilities, equipment and winter protection are real costs on large jobs.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Working with condo and HOA boards</h2>
<p>Associations have a duty to their members and usually a master policy with a large deductible. We help boards understand what the master policy pays, what falls to unit owners, and whether the gap justifies appraisal. We put findings in plain written summaries a board can share at a meeting.</p>
<h2>Business income is a separate question</h2>
<p>Lost income and extra expense can depend on the repair timeline. A realistic building estimate supports a realistic period of restoration, but we do not calculate income losses. If your claim includes them, we coordinate with your accountant or forensic consultant.</p>
<h2>Small commercial properties count too</h2>
<p>A general store with an apartment upstairs or a four-unit rental in Winooski is still a commercial claim. Our process scales down as well as up.</p>

<h2>Questions about this service</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do you work for condo unit owners or the association?</summary><div class="a"><p>Either, but never both on the same loss. We confirm who we represent in writing at the start.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal address business income?</summary><div class="a"><p>Some policies allow appraisal of business income amounts. Whether it applies depends on the wording, and we will tell you if it is outside our scope.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you need the building plans?</summary><div class="a"><p>They help but are not required. We measure what we need on site.</p></div></details></div>
